@end float

@noindent
The environment is crucial to the evaluation process, because it determines the
context in which an expression should be evaluated.  Indeed, one could say that
expressions in a programming language do not, in themselves, have any meaning.
Rather, an expression acquires a meaning only with respect to some environment
in which it is evaluated.  Even the interpretation of an expression as
straightforward as @code{x + 1} depends on an understanding that one is
operating in a context in which @code{+} is the name for addition and
@code{x} is bound somewhere within reach.  Thus, in our model of evaluation we
will always speak of evaluating an expression with respect to some environment.
To describe our programs, we will suppose that there is a global environment,
consisting of a single frame (with no enclosing environment) that holds the
bindings every expression in the program can use without any further local
definition: the standard library's names, and the module's own earlier
top-level definitions.  For example, the idea that @code{+} stands for integer
addition is captured by saying that the name @code{+} is bound in the global
environment to the standard library's addition function, and after the
definitions of @ref{3.1.1}, the name @code{withdraw} is bound in the global
environment to the withdrawal procedure.

OCaml builds its function values in exactly the shape this structure
describes.  Every closure carries its code together with the environment it
was created in, and every name the code reads is resolved in that environment,
frame by frame, exactly as the figure shows.  What an OCaml program cannot do
is inspect this structure.  The layout of a closure is an implementation
detail of the compiler, not a language guarantee, and no expression can ask a
function value for its environment.@footnote{The model of this section is
therefore judged by what it predicts about observable behavior: which cell a
name designates, and when the contents of that cell change.  Every such
prediction is a guarantee of the language.  Claims about the physical layout
of frames and closures are not, and OCaml's compiler is free to arrange them
however it likes, as long as the observable behavior stays the same.}  The
diagrams in this section are the model's diagrams, not a description of
run-time data structures.

