@node	3.2.1, 3.2.2, 3.2, 3.2
@subsection The Rules for Evaluation

The overall specification of how a program evaluates a combination
remains the same as when we first introduced it in @ref{1.1.3}:

@itemize @bullet

@item
To evaluate a combination:

@end itemize

@enumerate 1

@item
Evaluate the subexpressions of the combination.@footnote{Assignment introduces a
subtlety into step 1 of the evaluation rule.  As shown in @ref{Exercise 3.8},
the presence of assignment allows us to write expressions that will produce
different values depending on the order in which the subexpressions in a
combination are evaluated.  OCaml leaves this order unspecified, both for the
arguments of a function call and for the operands of operators such as
@code{+}, so the subtlety is not a Scheme curiosity but a fact of this
language too.  The order should always be considered to be an implementation
detail, and one should never write programs that depend on some particular
order.  For instance, a sophisticated compiler might optimize a program by
varying the order in which subexpressions are evaluated.}

@item
Apply the value of the operator subexpression to the values of the operand
subexpressions.

@end enumerate

@noindent
The environment model of evaluation replaces the substitution model in
specifying what it means to apply a compound procedure to arguments.

In the environment model of evaluation, a procedure is always a pair consisting
of some code and a pointer to an environment.  OCaml's function values have
exactly this shape: a closure carries the code of its @code{fun} expression
together with the environment in which that expression was evaluated.
Procedures are created in one way only: by evaluating a @code{fun} expression.
This produces a closure whose code is obtained from the text of the
@code{fun} expression and whose environment is the environment in which the
@code{fun} expression was evaluated to produce the closure.  For example,
consider the procedure definition

@example ocaml
let square x = x * x
@end example

@noindent
evaluated in the global environment.  The definition syntax is just syntactic
sugar for an underlying @code{fun} expression.  It would have been equivalent
to have used

@example ocaml
let square = fun x -> x * x
@end example

@noindent
which evaluates @code{fun x -> x * x} and binds @code{square} to the resulting
closure, all in the global environment.

@ref{Figure 3.2} shows the result of evaluating this @code{let} definition.
The closure is a pair whose code specifies that the function has one formal
parameter, namely @code{x}, and a function body @code{x * x}.  The environment
part of the closure is a pointer to the global environment, since that is the
environment in which the @code{fun} expression was evaluated to produce the
closure.  A new binding, which associates the closure with the name
@code{square}, has been added to the global frame.  In general, a @code{let}
definition creates definitions by adding bindings to the current frame.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.2}
@ifinfo
@quotation
@strong{Figure 3.2:} Environment structure produced by
evaluating @code{let square x = x * x} in the global environment.

@example
           +--------------------------------+
           | other variables                |
global --->|                                |
env        | square: --+                    |
           +-----------|--------------------+
                       |       ^
                       |       |
                       V       |
                .--------------|--.
                | closure      |  |
                |  code:       |  |
                |   fun x -> x * x |
                |  env:   o----+--+
                '------------------'
@end example
@end quotation
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.2b,,81mm,Figure 3.2: Environment structure produced by evaluating (define (square x) (* x x)) in the global environment.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.2b.std,,81mm,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.2:} Environment structure produced by evaluating @code{let square x = x * x} in the global environment.}
@end float

@noindent
Now that we have seen how closures are created, we can describe how they are
applied.  The environment model specifies: To apply a procedure to arguments,
create a new environment containing a frame that binds the parameters to the
values of the arguments.  The enclosing environment of this frame is the
environment specified by the closure.  Now, within this new environment,
evaluate the function body.

To show how this rule is followed, @ref{Figure 3.3} illustrates the environment
structure created by evaluating the expression @code{square 5} in the global
environment, where @code{square} is the closure generated in @ref{Figure 3.2}.
Applying the closure results in the creation of a new environment, labeled E1
in the figure, that begins with a frame in which @code{x}, the formal
parameter for the function, is bound to the argument 5.  The pointer leading
upward from this frame shows that the frame's enclosing environment is the
global environment.  The global environment is chosen here, because this is
the environment that is indicated as part of the @code{square} closure.
Within E1, we evaluate the body of the function, @code{x * x}.  Since the
value of @code{x} in E1 is 5, the result is @code{5 * 5}, or 25.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.3}
@ifinfo
@quotation
@strong{Figure 3.3:} Environment created by evaluating
@code{square 5} in the global environment.

@example
          +------------------------------------+
          | other variables                    |
global -->|                                    |
env       | square: --+                        |
          +-----------|---------------------+--+
                      |       ^             ^
                      |       |             |
                      V       |             |
                .-----------. |         +---+--+
                | closure   | |   E1 -->| x: 5 |
                | code: fun | |         +------+
                |  x -> x * x |
                | env: o-----+-+
                '-----------'
@end example
@end quotation
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.3b,,129mm,Figure 3.3: Environment created by evaluating (square 5) in the global environment.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.3b.std,,129mm,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.3:} Environment created by evaluating @code{square 5} in the global environment.}
@end float

@noindent
The environment model of procedure application can be summarized by two rules:

@itemize @bullet

@item
A procedure object is applied to a set of arguments by constructing a frame,
binding the formal parameters of the procedure to the arguments of the call,
and then evaluating the body of the procedure in the context of the new
environment constructed.  The new frame has as its enclosing environment the
environment part of the closure being applied.

@item
A procedure is created by evaluating a @code{fun} expression relative to a
given environment.  The resulting closure is a pair consisting of the text of
the @code{fun} expression and a pointer to the environment in which the
closure was created.

@end itemize

@noindent
We also specify that defining a name with a @code{let} definition creates a
binding in the current environment frame and assigns to the name the indicated
value.@footnote{A second definition of the same name does not change the
existing binding; it shadows it, in the sense of @ref{Figure 3.1}, adding a
new binding that hides the old one for the rest of the scope.  Anything that
captured the first binding, such as a closure built before the new definition,
keeps reaching that first binding, so shadowing is not a hidden way to change
what existing closures see.  What OCaml does not let a program do at all is
change a binding once it exists.  Mutation goes through @code{ref} cells and
@code{:=}, which makes every assignment visible in the type of the thing that
gets mutated.}  Finally, we specify the behavior of the operation that forced
us to introduce the environment model in the first place.  OCaml has no direct
counterpart of Scheme's @code{set!}, which changes the binding of a variable
in place.  What plays its role is the ref-cell assignment
@code{balance := !balance - amount} of @ref{3.1.1}.  Evaluating such an
assignment in some environment locates the binding of the name in the
environment and changes the cell that the binding designates to indicate the
new value.  That is, one finds the first frame in the environment that
contains a binding for the name and modifies the contents of the ref cell it
points to; the binding itself stays where it was.  If the name is unbound in
the environment, or bound to something that is not a ref cell, the program
does not type-check: OCaml rejects it before the program runs, where Scheme's
@code{set!} signals an error while the program is running.

These evaluation rules, though considerably more complex than the substitution
model, are still reasonably straightforward.  Moreover, the evaluation model,
though abstract, provides a correct description of how the language evaluates
expressions.  In @ref{Chapter 4} we shall see how this model can serve as a
blueprint for implementing a working interpreter.  The following sections
elaborate the details of the model by analyzing some illustrative programs.

@node	3.2.2, 3.2.3, 3.2.1, 3.2
@subsection Applying Simple Procedures

When we introduced the substitution model in @ref{1.1.5} we showed how
the combination @code{f 5} evaluates to 136, given the following procedure
definitions:

@example ocaml
let square x = x * x

let sum_of_squares x y = square x + square y

let f a = sum_of_squares (a + 1) (a * 2)
;;
@end example

@noindent
We can analyze the same example using the environment model.  @ref{Figure 3.4}
shows the three closures created by evaluating the definitions of
@code{f}, @code{square}, and @code{sum_of_squares} in the global environment.
Each closure consists of some code, together with a pointer to the global
environment.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.4}
@ifinfo
@strong{Figure 3.4:} Procedure objects in the global frame.

@example
          +--------------------------------------------------+
          | sum_of_squares:                                  |
global -->| square:                                          |
env       | f: --+                                           |
          +------|---------------------+----------------+----+
                 |     ^               |     ^          |     ^
                 |     |               |     |          |     |
                 V     |               V     |          V     |
             .---.---. |           .---.---. |        .---.---. |
             | O | O-+-+           | O | O-+-+        | O | O-+-+
             `-|-^---'            `-|-^---'         `-|-^---'
               |                    |                  |
               V                    V                  V
   parameters: a          parameters: x    parameters: x, y
   body: sum_of_squares   body: x * x      body: square x
           (a + 1)                           + square y
           (a * 2)
@end example
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.4b,,132mm,Figure 3.4: Procedure objects in the global frame.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.4b.std,,132mm,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.4:} Procedure objects in the global frame.}
@end float

@noindent
In @ref{Figure 3.5} we see the environment structure created by evaluating the
expression @code{f 5}.  The call to @code{f} creates a new environment E1
beginning with a frame in which @code{a}, the formal parameter of @code{f}, is
bound to the argument 5.  In E1, we evaluate the body of @code{f}:

@example ocaml
sum_of_squares (a + 1) (a * 2)
@end example

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.5}
@ifinfo
@strong{Figure 3.5:} Environments created by evaluating
@code{f 5} using the procedures in @ref{Figure 3.4}.

@example
          +-----------------------------------------------------+
global -->|                                                     |
env       +-----------------------------------------------------+
            ^              ^                ^               ^
f 5         |              |                |               |
        +------+       +-------+        +------+        +-------+
  E1 -->| a: 5 |  E2 ->| x: 6  |  E3 -->| x: 6 |  E4 -->| x: 10 |
        |      |       | y: 10 |        |      |        |       |
        +------+       +-------+        +------+        +-------+
   sum_of_squares   square x         x * x           x * x
     (a + 1)        + square y
     (a * 2)
@end example
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.5b,,142mm,Figure 3.5: Environments created by evaluating (f 5) using the procedures in Figure 3.4.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.5b.std,,142mm,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.5:} Environments created by evaluating @code{f 5} @w{using} the procedures in @ref{Figure 3.4}.}
@end float

@noindent
To evaluate this combination, we first evaluate the subexpressions.  The first
subexpression, @code{sum_of_squares}, has a value that is a closure.
(Notice how this value is found: We first look in the first frame of E1, which
contains no binding for @code{sum_of_squares}.  Then we proceed to the
enclosing environment, i.e. the global environment, and find the binding shown
in @ref{Figure 3.4}.)  The other two subexpressions are evaluated by applying
the primitive operations @code{+} and @code{*} to evaluate the two combinations
@code{a + 1} and @code{a * 2} to obtain 6 and 10, respectively.

@noindent
Now we apply the closure @code{sum_of_squares} to the arguments 6 and
10.  This results in a new environment E2 in which the formal parameters
@code{x} and @code{y} are bound to the arguments.  Within E2 we evaluate the
combination @code{square x + square y}.  This leads us to evaluate
@code{square x}, where @code{square} is found in the global frame and
@code{x} is 6.  Once again, we set up a new environment, E3, in which @code{x}
is bound to 6, and within this we evaluate the body of @code{square}, which is
@code{x * x}.  Also as part of applying @code{sum_of_squares}, we must
evaluate the subexpression @code{square y}, where @code{y} is 10.  This
second call to @code{square} creates another environment, E4, in which
@code{x}, the formal parameter of @code{square}, is bound to 10.  And within E4
we must evaluate @code{x * x}.

The important point to observe is that each call to @code{square} creates a new
environment containing a binding for @code{x}.  We can see here how the
different frames serve to keep separate the different local variables all named
@code{x}.  Notice that each frame created by @code{square} points to the global
environment, since this is the environment indicated by the @code{square}
closure.

After the subexpressions are evaluated, the results are returned.  The values
generated by the two calls to @code{square} are added by
@code{sum_of_squares}, and this result is returned by @code{f}.  Since our
focus here is on the environment structures, we will not dwell on how these
returned values are passed from call to call; however, this is also an
important aspect of the evaluation process, and we will return to it in detail
in @ref{Chapter 5}.

@quotation
@emph{SICP exercise 3.9 is about Scheme itself, so this edition replaces it;
the exercise below keeps the number and teaches the same section material.}
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.9}Exercise 3.9:} In @ref{1.2.1} we analyzed two
procedures for computing factorials, a recursive version

@example ocaml
let rec factorial n =
  if n = 1
  then 1
  else n * factorial (n - 1)
@end example

@noindent
and an iterative version

@example ocaml
let factorial_iter n =
  let rec fact_iter product counter =
    if counter > n
    then product
    else fact_iter (counter * product)
                   (counter + 1)
  in
  fact_iter 1 1
;;
@end example

@noindent
Draw the growth of the call stack that evaluating @code{factorial 6} produces
under each version.  The first version must keep one frame alive for each
pending multiplication, so the stack grows linearly with @code{n}.  The
second version's own call is its last action, so its frame takes the
caller's place and the stack stays constant.  Instrument both versions so
that each call returns the value it computes together with the depth of the
deepest call frame that was live at any point during that call: the
recursive instrument hands each nested call a depth one greater than its
own, because the pending multiplication keeps the caller's frame alive,
while the iterative instrument hands its tail call its own depth, because
the callee's frame takes the caller's place.  Check the instrumented runs
against your drawing: @code{factorial_traced 6} should report a deepest
depth of 6, and @code{factorial_iter_traced 6} a deepest depth of 1; then
run the uninstrumented iterative version at @code{n = 1000000}, a size at
which the recursive version's own frames would long since have exhausted
the stack, to see the guarantee deliver constant space in practice.@footnote{The
environment model will not clarify our claim in @ref{1.2.1} that a procedure
such as @code{fact_iter} can execute in a constant amount of space using tail
recursion.  OCaml's compiler does guarantee that a call in tail position
reuses the caller's frame, so the guarantee is a fact of this language and not
merely of Scheme implementations; what the environment model cannot yet
explain is how a compiler delivers it.  We will discuss the control structure
behind that delivery in @ref{5.4}.}
@end quotation

@node	3.2.3, 3.2.4, 3.2.2, 3.2
@subsection Frames as the Repository of Local State

We can turn to the environment model to see how procedures and assignment can
be used to represent objects with local state.  As an example, consider the
``withdrawal processor'' from @ref{3.1.1} created by calling the
procedure

@example ocaml
let make_withdraw balance =
  let balance = ref balance in
  fun amount ->
    if !balance >= amount
    then (
      balance := !balance - amount;
      Balance !balance)
    else Insufficient_funds
;;
@end example

@noindent
Let us describe the evaluation of

@example ocaml
let w1 = make_withdraw 100;;
@end example

@noindent
followed by

@example ocaml
w1 50;;
(* => Balance 50 *)
@end example

@noindent
@ref{Figure 3.6} shows the result of defining the @code{make_withdraw}
function in the global environment.  This produces a closure that contains a
pointer to the global environment.  So far, this is no different from the
examples we have already seen, except that the body of the function is itself
a @code{fun} expression under a @code{let} binding.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.6}
@ifinfo
@quotation
@strong{Figure 3.6:} Result of defining @code{make_withdraw} in the global environment.

@example
          +---------------------------+
global -->| make_withdraw: --+        |
env       +------------------|--------+
                             |      ^
                             V      |
                    .--------------------.
                    | closure            |
                    |  code:             |
                    |   fun balance ->   |
                    |     let balance =  |
                    |       ref balance  |
                    |     in fun amount  |
                    |       -> ...       |
                    |  env: global       |
                    '--------------------'
@end example
@end quotation
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.6c,,128mm,Figure 3.6: Result of defining make-withdraw in the global environment.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.6c.std,,128mm,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.6:} Result of defining @code{make_withdraw} in the global environment.}
@end float

@noindent
The interesting part of the computation happens when we apply the function
@code{make_withdraw} to an argument.  We begin, as usual, by setting up an
environment E1 in which the formal parameter @code{balance} is bound to the
argument 100.  Within this environment, we evaluate the body of
@code{make_withdraw}, namely the @code{let} binding and the @code{fun}
expression under it.  The @code{let} binding allocates a fresh @code{ref}
cell, initializes it with 100, and adds a binding of @code{balance} to the
frame of E1, shadowing the parameter of the same name.  The @code{fun}
expression then constructs a new closure whose code is as specified by the
@code{fun} and whose environment is E1, so the fresh cell is inside the
environment the closure carries.  This closure is the value returned by the
call to @code{make_withdraw}.  It is bound to @code{w1} in the global
environment, since the @code{let} definition itself is being evaluated in the
global environment.  @ref{Figure 3.7} shows the resulting structure.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.7}
@ifinfo
@quotation
@strong{Figure 3.7:} Result of evaluating @code{let w1 = make_withdraw 100}.

@example
          +------------------------------------------+
global -->| make_withdraw: ---+   w1: --+            |
env       +-------------------|---------|------------+
              ^               |         |     ^
              |               V         |     |
              |       .---------------. |     |
              |       | closure       | |     |
              |       |  code:        | |     |
              |       |   fun amount  | |     |
              |       |     -> ...    | |     |
              |       |  env:  o      | |     |
              |       '-------|-|-----' |     |
              |               | |       |     |
              |               | V       |     |
              |               | +--------------------+
              |               | | E1                 |
              |               | |  balance: o        |
              |               | +--------|-----------+
              |               |          |
              |               |          V
              |               |   +----------------+
              |               |   | ref cell       |
              |               |   |  contents: 100 |
              |               |   +----------------+
@end example
@end quotation
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.7b,,145mm,Figure 3.7: Result of evaluating (define W1 (make-withdraw 100)).,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.7b.std,,145mm,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.7:} Result of evaluating @code{let w1 = make_withdraw 100}.}
@end float

@noindent
Now we can analyze what happens when the closure is applied to an argument:

@example ocaml
w1 50;;
(* => Balance 50 *)
@end example

@noindent
We begin by constructing a frame in which the formal parameter of the
closure, @code{amount}, is bound to the argument 50.  The crucial point to
observe is that this frame is not the global environment but rather the
environment E1, because this is the environment that is specified by the
@code{w1} closure.  Within this new environment, we evaluate the body of the
closure.  The expression being evaluated references both @code{amount} and
@code{balance}; @code{amount} will be found in the first frame in the
environment, while @code{balance} will be found by following the enclosing
environment pointer to E1, where the name is bound to the ref cell.  When the
assignment @code{balance := !balance - amount} is executed, the binding of
@code{balance} in E1 stays exactly where it is; what changes is the contents
of the ref cell that the binding designates.  The resulting structure is
shown in @ref{Figure 3.8}.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.8}
@ifinfo
@quotation
@strong{Figure 3.8:} Environments created by applying the closure bound to @code{w1}.

@example
          +------------------------------------------+
global -->| make_withdraw: ---+   w1: --+            |
env       +-------------------|---------|------------+
              ^               |         |     ^
              |               V         |     |
              |       .---------------. |     |
              |       | closure       | |     |
              |       |  code:        | |     |
              |       |   fun amount  | |     |
              |       |     -> ...    | |     |
              |       |  env:  o      | |     |
              |       '-------|-|-----' |     |
              |               | |       |     |
   amount: 50 |               | V       |     |
   +--------+ |               | +--------------------+
   | E2       | |               | | E1                 |
   +--------+-+ |               | |  balance: o        |
                |               | +--------|-----------+
                |               |          |
                |               |          V
                |               |   +----------------+
                |               |   | ref cell       |
                |               |   |  contents: 50  |
                |               |   +----------------+
@end example
@end quotation
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.8c,,145mm,Figure 3.8: Environments created by applying the procedure object W1.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.8c.std,,145mm,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.8:} Environments created by applying the closure bound to @code{w1}.}
@end float

@noindent
When the @code{:=} is executed, the contents of the ref cell in E1 is
changed.  The frame that contains the binding of @code{amount} is no longer
relevant once the call has terminated, since the call that constructed it has
terminated, and there are no pointers to that frame from other parts of the
environment.  The next time @code{w1} is called, this will build a new frame
that binds @code{amount} and whose enclosing environment is E1.  We see that
the ref cell bound in E1 serves as the ``place'' that holds the local state
variable for the closure bound to @code{w1}.  @ref{Figure 3.9} shows the
situation after the call to @code{w1}.@footnote{Whether the compiled program
really keeps the frame E1 alive, or keeps only the ref cell and drops the
rest, is an implementation question the language does not answer.  The
guarantee is the one the model predicts: every call of @code{w1} reads and
writes this same cell, and no other call can reach it.  Nothing in a program
can observe the frame itself.}

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.9}
@ifinfo
@strong{Figure 3.9:} Environments after the call to @code{w1}.

@example
          +------------------------------------------+
global -->| make_withdraw: ---+   w1: --+            |
env       +-------------------|---------|------------+
              ^               |         |     ^
              |               V         |     |
              |       .---------------. |     |
              |       | closure       | |     |
              |       |  code:        | |     |
              |       |   fun amount  | |     |
              |       |     -> ...    | |     |
              |       |  env:  o      | |     |
              |       '-------|-|-----' |     |
              |               | |       |     |
              |               | V       |     |
              |               | +--------------------+
              |               | | E1                 |
              |               | |  balance: o        |
              |               | +--------|-----------+
              |               |          |
              |               |          V
              |               |   +----------------+
              |               |   | ref cell       |
              |               |   |  contents: 50  |
              |               |   +----------------+
@end example
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.9b,,147mm,Figure 3.9: Environments after the call to W1.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.9b.std,,147mm,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.9:} Environments after the call to @code{w1}.}
@end float

@noindent
Nothing in this arrangement belongs to withdrawal closures in particular.  A
ref cell can be shared by any bundle of closures that are created in the
scope where the cell is bound.  Here, for example, one cell backs two
closures, a @code{bump} that increments the count and a @code{peek} that
reads it:

@example ocaml
let make_shared_counter () =
  let count = ref 0 in
  let bump () =
    count := !count + 1;
    !count
  in
  let peek () = !count in
  (bump, peek)
;;

let bump, peek = make_shared_counter ();;

bump ();;
(* => 1 *)

bump ();;
(* => 2 *)

peek ();;
(* => 2 *)
@end example

@noindent
Both closures carry E1, the scope of the single @code{let} binding, so both
reach the same cell, and every @code{bump} is visible to the next
@code{peek}.  By contrast, two separate calls to a one-closure counter each
allocate a cell of their own:

@example ocaml
let make_counter () =
  let count = ref 0 in
  fun () ->
    count := !count + 1;
    !count
;;

let c1 = make_counter ();;
let c2 = make_counter ();;

c1 ();;
(* => 1 *)

c1 ();;
(* => 2 *)

c2 ();;
(* => 1 *)
@end example

@noindent
The two calls of @code{c1} observe each other; the call of @code{c2} does not
observe either of them.  The cell a closure captures, and not the closure's
code, decides which objects share state.

Observe what happens when we create a second ``withdrawal'' object by making
another call to @code{make_withdraw}:

@example ocaml
let w2 = make_withdraw 100;;
@end example

@noindent
This produces the structure of @ref{Figure 3.10}, which shows that
@code{w2} is a closure, that is, a pair with some code and an environment.
The environment E1' of @code{w2} was created by the call to
@code{make_withdraw}.  It contains its own local binding for
@code{balance}, a fresh ref cell with its own contents 100.  On the other
hand, @code{w1} and @code{w2} have the same code: the code specified by the
@code{fun} expression in the body of @code{make_withdraw}.@footnote{In
OCaml this sharing of code is literal: the compiler translates the body of
@code{make_withdraw} once, and every closure it returns points at that one
translated body, differing only in the environment they carry.  The
interpreter we build in @ref{Chapter 4} shares the code the same way.}  We
see here why @code{w1} and @code{w2} behave as independent objects.  Calls
to @code{w1} reference the ref cell that is bound in E1, whereas calls to
@code{w2} reference the ref cell bound in E1'.  Changes to each thus do not
affect the other.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.10}
@ifinfo
@strong{Figure 3.10:} Using @code{let w2 = make_withdraw 100} to
create a second object.

@example
