global -->| make_withdraw: ---+  w1: --+  w2: --+     |
env       +-------------------|--------|--------|-----+
              ^               |        |        |   ^
              |               V        |        |   |
              |    .---------------.   |        |   |
              |    | closure       |   |        |   |
              |    |  code: o-----------+--------+---+
              |    |  env: global  |   |        |
              |    '---------------'   |        |
              |           ^            |        |
              |           |            V        V
              |           |    .---------------.  .---------------.
              |           |    | closure       |  | closure       |
              |           |    |  code: o      |  |  code: o      |
              |           |    |  env: o       |  |  env: o       |
              |           |    '-------|------'  '-------|------'
              |           |            |                 |
              |           |            V                 V
              |           |    +---------------+  +---------------+
              |           |    | E1: balance o |  | E1': balance o|
              |           |    '-------|-----'  '-------|-----'
              |           |            |                 |
              |           |            V                 V
              |           |    +---------------+  +---------------+
              |           |    | ref cell 100  |  | ref cell 100  |
              |           |    +---------------+  +---------------+
@end example
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.10b,,147mm,Figure 3.10: Using (define W2 (make-withdraw 100)) to create a second object.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.10b.std,,147mm,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.10:} Using @code{let w2 = make_withdraw 100} to create a second object.}
@end float

@quotation
@emph{SICP exercise 3.10 is about Scheme itself, so this edition replaces it;
the exercise below keeps the number and teaches the same section material.}
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.10}Exercise 3.10:} In the @code{make_withdraw}
procedure, the local state is created by the @code{let} binding

@example ocaml
let make_withdraw balance =
  let balance = ref balance in
  fun amount ->
    if !balance >= amount
    then (
      balance := !balance - amount;
      Balance !balance)
    else Insufficient_funds
;;
@end example

@noindent
The book's Scheme original contrasts two writings of @code{make-withdraw},
one whose parameter stays a plain binding and one wrapped in an explicit
@code{let}; in OCaml a mutable local has to be built somewhere, and the
@code{let} above is that place: it is where the cell is allocated, while the
@code{fun} under it is where the cell gets captured.  Trace the lifetime of
the cell by writing @code{make_withdraw_traced}, an instrumented variant of
@code{make_withdraw} that returns, next to the withdrawal closure, the cell
it built:

@example ocaml
type withdraw_with_cell =
  { withdraw : int -> withdraw_result
  ; balance_cell : int ref
  }
@end example

@noindent
Use physical equality (@code{==}, which is true when its two operands are the
very same cell) to show that the cell captured by @code{w1} is the very cell
that @code{make_withdraw_traced} returned: change the cell's contents
directly between two calls and watch the next withdrawal see the change.
Then create a second object with @code{let w2 = make_withdraw_traced 100}
and show that the two accounts hold two different cells, although the two
cells start with the same contents.  Finally, draw the cell's lifetime as a
timeline: at which expression is it allocated, which closures capture it,
what happens to the call's own frame E1 when @code{make_withdraw_traced}
returns, and why does the cell outlive that frame?
@end quotation

@node	3.2.4, 3.3, 3.1, 3.2
@subsection Internal Definitions

Section @ref{1.1.8} introduced the idea that procedures can have internal
definitions, thus leading to a block structure as in the following procedure to
compute square roots:

@example ocaml
let sqrt x =
  let good_enough guess =
    Float.abs (guess *. guess -. x) < 0.001
  in
  let improve guess = (guess +. x /. guess) /. 2.0 in
  let rec sqrt_iter guess =
    if good_enough guess
    then guess
    else sqrt_iter (improve guess)
  in
  sqrt_iter 1.0
;;
@end example

@noindent
Now we can use the environment model to see why these internal definitions
behave as desired.  @ref{Figure 3.11} shows the point in the evaluation of the
expression @code{sqrt 2.0} where the internal function @code{good_enough} has
been called for the first time with @code{guess} equal to 1.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.11}
@ifinfo
@strong{Figure 3.11:} @code{Sqrt} procedure with internal definitions.

@example
          +--------------------------------------------------+
global -->| sqrt: --+                                        |
env       |         |                                        |
          +---------|----------------------------------------+
                    V       ^                   ^
                .---.---.   |                   |
     +----------+-O | O-+---+        +----------+------------+
     |          `---^---'            | x: 2.0                |
     V                         E1 -->| good_enough: -+      |
parameters: x                       | improve: ...  |      |
body: let good_enough guess = ...   | sqrt_iter: ...|      |
      let improve guess = ...       +----------------|------+
      let rec sqrt_iter guess = ...  ^  ^            |     ^
      sqrt_iter 1.0                  |  |            V     |
                            +---------++ |        .---.---. |
                      E2 -->| guess: 1 | |        | O | O-+-+
                            +----------+ |        `-|-^---'
                      call to sqrt_iter  |          |
                                         |          V
                               +---------++    parameters: guess
                         E3 -->| guess: 1 |    body: Float.abs (...
                               +----------+             ...)
                         call to good_enough
@end example
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.11b,,147mm,Figure 3.11: Sqrt procedure with internal definitions.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.11b.std,,147mm,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.11:} @code{Sqrt} procedure with internal definitions.}
@end float

Observe the structure of the environment.  @code{Sqrt} is a name in the
global environment that is bound to a closure whose associated environment is
the global environment.  When @code{sqrt} was called, a new environment E1
was formed, subordinate to the global environment, in which the parameter
@code{x} is bound to 2.0.  The body of @code{sqrt} was then evaluated in E1.
Since the first expression in the body of @code{sqrt} is

@example ocaml
let good_enough guess =
  Float.abs (guess *. guess -. x) < 0.001
@end example

@noindent
evaluating this expression bound the name @code{good_enough} in the first
frame of E1, to a closure whose associated environment is E1.  Similarly,
@code{improve} and @code{sqrt_iter} were bound as closures in E1.  For
conciseness, @ref{Figure 3.11} shows only the closure for
@code{good_enough}.@footnote{Scheme's internal definitions may appear in any
order and may refer to one another; OCaml's @code{let @dots{} in} bindings
are evaluated in order and see only the bindings written before them.  The
body of @code{sqrt} above is already in an order OCaml accepts, and
@code{sqrt_iter} refers to @code{good_enough} and @code{improve} through E1
exactly as the figure shows.  Internal functions that must call one another
mutually are written as one @code{let rec @dots{} and @dots{}} group, which
OCaml compiles to mutually recursive closures sharing the same frame.}

@noindent
After the internal functions were defined, the expression
@code{sqrt_iter 1.0} was evaluated, still in environment E1.  So the closure
bound to @code{sqrt_iter} in E1 was called with 1.0 as an argument.  This
created an environment E2 in which @code{guess}, the parameter of
@code{sqrt_iter}, is bound to 1.  @code{Sqrt_iter} in turn called
@code{good_enough} with the value of @code{guess} (from E2) as the argument
for @code{good_enough}.  This set up another environment, E3, in which
@code{guess} (the parameter of @code{good_enough}) is bound to 1.  Although
@code{sqrt_iter} and @code{good_enough} both have a parameter named
@code{guess}, these are two distinct local variables located in different
frames.  Also, E2 and E3 both have E1 as their enclosing environment, because
the closures bound to @code{sqrt_iter} and @code{good_enough} both have E1 as
their environment part.  One consequence of this is that the name @code{x}
that appears in the body of @code{good_enough} will reference the binding of
@code{x} that appears in E1, namely the value of @code{x} with which the
original @code{sqrt} procedure was called.

The environment model thus explains the two key properties that make local
procedure definitions a useful technique for modularizing programs:

@itemize @bullet

@item
The names of the local procedures do not interfere with names external to the
enclosing procedure, because the local names are bound in the frame that the
enclosing procedure creates when it is run, rather than being bound in the
global environment.  OCaml enforces the same discipline statically: a local
@code{let} binding shadows any outer name of the same spelling for the rest
of its scope, without touching the outer binding.

@item
The local procedures can access the arguments of the enclosing procedure,
simply by using parameter names as free variables.  This is because the body
of the local function is evaluated in an environment that is subordinate to
the evaluation environment for the enclosing procedure; in OCaml's terms, the
internal closures capture the frame of the enclosing call, and the compiler
keeps that frame's contents alive exactly as long as some closure can still
reach them.

@end itemize

@quotation
@emph{SICP exercise 3.11 is about Scheme itself, so this edition replaces it;
the exercise below keeps the number and teaches the same section material.}
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.11}Exercise 3.11:} In @ref{3.2.3} we saw how the
environment model described the behavior of procedures with local state.  Now
we have seen how internal definitions work.  The bank account of
@ref{3.1.1} contains both of these aspects, packaged as a record of the
closures that operate on one captured cell:

@example ocaml
let make_account balance =
  let balance = ref balance in
  let withdraw amount =
    if !balance >= amount
    then (
      balance := !balance - amount;
      Balance !balance)
    else Insufficient_funds
  in
  let deposit amount =
    balance := !balance + amount;
    !balance
  in
  { withdraw; deposit }
;;
@end example

@noindent
Draw the structure generated by the sequence of interactions

@example ocaml
let acc = make_account 50;;

acc.deposit 40;;
(* => 90 *)

acc.withdraw 60;;
(* => Balance 30 *)
@end example

@noindent
showing which closures capture which cell, and where the local state for
@code{acc} is kept.  Suppose we define another account

@example ocaml
let acc2 = make_account 100;;
@end example

@noindent
How are the local states for the two accounts kept distinct?  Which parts of
the structure are shared between @code{acc} and @code{acc2}?
@end quotation

@quotation
@emph{Exercise 3.11a is added by this edition and extends exercise 3.11; SICP
numbers stop at 3.11.} @strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.11a}Exercise 3.11a:} The
accounts of exercise 3.11 raise the sameness question of @ref{3.1.3} in a
form OCaml answers with two different operators.  Structural equality
(@code{=}) compares the pieces of a value; physical equality (@code{==})
compares whether two names designate the very same object.  Let @code{acc}
and @code{twin} be accounts built by two separate calls to
@code{make_account 100}, and let @code{alias} be a second name for
@code{acc}, bound by @code{let alias = acc}.  Determine the value of

@example ocaml
acc == twin;;
acc == alias;;
acc = twin;;
@end example

@noindent
and of the behavioral test of sameness: withdraw 20 through @code{acc} and
through @code{twin}, and compare the two answers with @code{=}.  Say, for
each result, which relationship from your drawing in exercise 3.11 it
reflects.@footnote{OCaml's structural comparison cannot compare functional
values at all: @code{acc = twin} raises @code{Invalid_argument} at run time,
because two closures can be told apart only by where they were created, and
no structural traversal reaches that.  For values that carry closures, such
as account records, @code{==} is the only comparison the language provides.}
@end quotation
